commit 358f6a875afa154f9eb04411c0bc8288b2f1082a
Author:     Jeong, YunWon <69878+youknowone@users.noreply.github.com>
AuthorDate: Sat Sep 19 19:21:32 2026 +0900
Commit:     GitHub <noreply@github.com>
CommitDate: Sat Sep 19 19:21:32 2026 +0900

    native _json, _pickle, pyexpat, and _elementtree (#8741)
    
    * _json: add native make_encoder (c_make_encoder)
    
    json.encoder fell back to the pure-Python _make_iterencode because _json
    had no make_encoder, making json.dumps ~25x slower than CPython. Implement
    the C encoder semantics natively: str/None/bool/int/float/list/tuple/dict
    ordering, key coercion and skipkeys, sort_keys via the VM's fallible sort
    (comparison errors propagate instead of panicking), circular reference
    detection, indent handling, allow_nan, default() fallback with recursion
    guard, and CPython's add_note annotations. Removes the now-passing
    expectedFailure markers in test_json.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * pyexpat: add UseForeignDTD, fix silently swallowed handler exceptions
    
    genshi's XML template loader (bm_genshi) called xmlparser.UseForeignDTD(),
    which pyexpat.rs didn't implement, raising AttributeError. Add it as an
    accepted no-op: the pyexpat module here wraps the pure-Rust xml-rs crate
    (not real libexpat), which has no hook for splicing in a foreign DTD.
    
    Root-causing the resulting bug also uncovered why genshi's template
    generate()/render() produced empty output instead of erroring: do_parse's
    invoke_handler swallowed every exception raised by a Python callback
    (handler.call(...).ok()), and genshi's expat callbacks fail immediately
    by reading `parser.CurrentLineNumber`/`CurrentColumnNumber`, which
    pyexpat.rs never exposed. The AttributeError was silently discarded,
    so no start/end/text events ever reached genshi's queue.
    
    Fix both: propagate handler exceptions out of Parse()/ParseFile() (matching
    libexpat/CPython semantics), and add CurrentLineNumber/CurrentColumnNumber/
    CurrentByteIndex as read-only attributes backed by xml-rs's per-event
    TextPosition (byte index is derived from it and is exact for ASCII
    documents). This also fixes test_minidom's
    testExceptionOnSpacesInXMLNSValue, previously an expected failure because
    the ValueError from an invalid xmlns value was swallowed the same way.
    
    PositionTest in test_pyexpat.py checks byte-exact expat semantics (token
    *start* position) that xml-rs's *current parse cursor* position can't
    reproduce exactly, so that one test is now marked an expected failure.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * pyexpat: support multi-chunk Parse()/ParseFile() feeds
    
    bm_xml_etree failed with AssertionError: missing end tags from
    TreeBuilder.close(), because xml.etree.ElementTree reads and feeds large
    documents to XMLParser.feed() in ~64KB chunks (and xml.sax's
    IncrementalParser and ElementTree's XMLPullParser do the same). Each
    Parse()/ParseFile() call built a brand-new xml-rs EventReader from only
    the bytes passed in that call, discarding all parser state between calls.
    When a chunk boundary fell in the middle of an element, the fragment
    parsed by itself was invalid/incomplete XML, so xml-rs silently errored
    out (matching this module's existing "ignore stricter xml-rs errors"
    behavior) and the corresponding start/end events were dropped entirely
    -- not just delayed.
    
    xml-rs has no API to resume a parser across separate Read sources, so
    instead each call now appends the new chunk to a persistent buffer and
    re-parses the buffer as a whole, replaying only the events at or beyond
    the count already dispatched in previous calls (dispatched_events). This
    also makes position tracking (CurrentLineNumber/Column/ByteIndex) correct
    regardless of how the input was chunked. Trade-off: each Parse() call is
    now O(size fed so far), so a document delivered in many small chunks is
    parsed several times over; acceptable for correctness but noticeably
    slower than a true incremental parser for pathological chunk sizes.
    
    This also fixes several previously-xfailed tests that depend on
    incremental feeding: test_xml_etree.py's XMLPullParserTest (test_events,
    test_feed_while_iterating, test_simple_xml, test_simple_xml_chunk_22,
    test_simple_xml_with_ns) and ElementTreeTest.test_parseliteral, plus
    test_sax.py's ExpatReaderTest (test_expat_entityresolver_default,
    test_expat_incremental, test_expat_incremental_reset) -- their
    expectedFailure markers are removed. test_simple_xml_chunk_1/_chunk_5
    (1- and 5-byte chunks) still fail and remain xfail: at that granularity
    xml-rs's stricter-than-expat tokenizer errors on some intermediate
    prefixes that libexpat tolerates.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * test: drop stale expectedFailure markers fixed by the pyexpat changes
    
    test_plistlib's four invalid-document tests and test_xmlrpc's
    test_loads_unsupported pass now that pyexpat no longer swallows
    handler exceptions and keeps parser state across chunked feeds.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * pyexpat: drive a single background EventReader for true incremental Parse()
    
    8802451f3 fixed multi-chunk Parse()/ParseFile() feeds by accumulating every
    fed byte and re-parsing the whole buffer from scratch on each call, which is
    O(total bytes) per chunk -- quadratic in document size. xml-rs's EventReader
    has no API to pause/resume across separate Read sources, so instead one
    EventReader is now driven to completion on a dedicated background thread fed
    through a condvar-backed queue (FeedBuffer/FeedReader); Parse()/ParseFile()
    push their chunk, wake the thread, and drain (on the calling thread only)
    whatever events that unblocks, stopping once the thread reports it is
    blocked waiting for more input. Handlers always run on the calling thread.
    A handler exception stops dispatch without tearing down the stream, so the
    next Parse() resumes where it left off, mirroring libexpat. CurrentByteIndex
    is now computed incrementally per line (with an amortized per-query cursor,
    needed since real documents are often a single very long line) instead of
    scanning the whole buffer. ParserCreate() never spawns a thread; one is
    spawned lazily by the first Parse()/ParseFile() call, and dropping the
    parser always stops and joins it.
    
    Fixes test_xml_etree{,_c}'s test_events_comment, previously xfailed because
    the old whole-buffer replay lost top-level comment events across feeds.
    
    Measured (M4 Pro, release build):
    - Scaling (ElementTree.parse, newline-separated doc): new backend 1/4/16 MB
      = 0.15/0.61/2.35s (linear); CPython 3.14 same file = 0.015/0.055/0.23s.
    - pyperformance's bm_xml_etree doc (156KB, effectively one line): old
      backend single etree.parse() = 1.99s, single iterparse() = 3.53s; new
      backend = 0.025s / 0.031s (~80-110x). Full bench_parse/iterparse/generate/
      process (30/10/10/1 reps) now finish in ~1.5s/0.6s/0.4s/0.3s instead of
      timing out past pyperformance's 400s limit.
    - 10k ParserCreate() (never fed / partially fed / fully fed and dropped):
      0.32s, no residual native threads, no RSS growth.
    
    test -j4 test_pyexpat test_xml_etree test_xml_etree_c test_sax test_minidom
    test_xml_dom_minicompat test_pulldom test_plistlib test_xmlrpc test_html
    test_htmlparser test_threading: all pass (1350 tests, 89 skipped).
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * pyexpat: add a Sync fallback backend for targets without threads
    
    2f58e9531 made pyexpat always drive its EventReader on a background
    thread (ParserStream::spawn), panicking via .expect() if the thread
    couldn't be spawned. pyexpat is registered unconditionally, including
    on wasm32, where std::thread::spawn always fails at runtime, so the
    first Parse() call panicked there.
    
    Introduce a Backend enum: Backend::Threaded (the existing ParserStream)
    on targets that can spawn threads, and a new Backend::Sync, the
    accumulate-and-reparse implementation from 8802451f3, used on wasm32
    and as a runtime fallback if spawning a thread ever fails. Both share
    LineTracker for identical CurrentLineNumber/CurrentColumnNumber/
    CurrentByteIndex and handler/error semantics.
    
    Fixed two correctness bugs while porting the Sync backend forward:
    marking the parser permanently "finished" as soon as a from-scratch
    reparse happened to reach EndDocument (which can be a false positive
    on a not-yet-complete buffer, e.g. right before epilog content
    arrives) rather than only on the caller's real isfinal signal; and
    counting that same EndDocument towards the dispatched-event index,
    which could make a real event land on an index a later, longer reparse
    had already skipped past. Verified by force-selecting Backend::Sync
    under a test-only env var (removed) and diffing its output against
    Backend::Threaded for the xml/sax/etree/plistlib/xmlrpc test suites
    and a 2MB document fed in 1KB chunks.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * _pickle: native Pickler, Unpickler and PickleBuffer
    
    Adds `crates/stdlib/src/pickle.rs`, a native `_pickle` implementing
    Pickler, Unpickler, PickleBuffer, PicklerMemoProxy/UnpicklerMemoProxy,
    dump/dumps/load/loads and the PickleError family, so `Lib/pickle.py`
    stops falling back to `_Pickler`/`_Unpickler`.
    
    Protocols 0-5 are supported and produce byte-identical output to the
    pure-Python pickler, including framing for protocol >= 4, memoization,
    `fast` mode, dispatch_table, reducer_override, persistent_id /
    persistent_load, find_class with `_compat_pickle` fix_imports, buffer
    callbacks and out-of-band protocol 5 buffers. This also turns on
    `pickle.PickleBuffer`, which RustPython did not have before.
    
    Speedups over the pure-Python implementation (best of 5, us/op):
    pickle 835 -> 21 (39x), pickle_list 525 -> 4.4 (119x),
    pickle_dict 4018 -> 32 (125x), unpickle 630 -> 24 (26x),
    unpickle_list 317 -> 5.0 (63x). CPython 3.14's C accelerator runs the
    same workloads 1.5-2.9x faster than this.
    
    test_pickle now runs 1008 tests (was 470) since the C-implementation
    test classes are enabled; test_pickle, test_pickletools,
    test_picklebuffer are green and 22 obsolete expectedFailure markers are
    removed.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * _pickle: raise the pickle.find_class audit event
    
    Unpickler.find_class now raises `sys.audit('pickle.find_class', module,
    name)` like CPython, so audit hooks can veto global lookups
    (test_audit.test_pickle). Also drops four expectedFailure markers that
    the native module makes pass: test_pydoc's builtin-method description
    tests, test_memoryview.test_picklebuffer_reference_loop and
    test_pyclbr.test_others.
    
    Full suite: 456 test files / 42,648 tests green.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * _pickle: avoid temporary buffers on the save fast paths
    
    Opcode plus payload is now appended straight to the output buffer
    (`write_pair`) instead of building a `Vec` per int, str, bytes and memo
    opcode, `save_tuple` no longer copies the element slice, and the
    `when serializing ...` note text is only formatted when an error is
    actually raised.
    
    bm_pickle "pickle" workload: 21.4 -> 17.0 us/op.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * pyexpat: make the threaded backend fork-safe
    
    fork() only keeps the calling thread. test_platform's
    test_mac_ver_with_fork crashed the child with SIGTRAP: plistlib parses
    a plist through pyexpat (spawning a background parser thread) before
    os.fork(), then parses again in the child. Bisecting away every other
    explanation (a plain threading.Thread survives the same fork fine,
    since it goes through this VM's own fork-safe thread bookkeeping)
    showed the crash is not from touching the *inherited* ParserStream
    (already joined by refcounting before the fork in this repro) but from
    spawning a *brand new* parser thread from the child, which reliably
    crashed during the new thread's own startup.
    
    Register a pthread_atfork child hook (lazily, the first time a parser
    thread is spawned) that sets a permanent, process-wide flag the
    instant this process is ever the child of a fork(). Once set:
    - ParserStream::try_spawn refuses to spawn any further thread and
      Backend::new falls back to Backend::Sync instead, exactly as on
      targets that cannot spawn threads at all.
    - Feeding an inherited Backend::Threaded raises a clear ExpatError
      instead of touching its FeedBuffer/channel (whose mutex/condvar may
      look held by a thread that no longer exists), and dropping it leaks
      its JoinHandle/buffer/channel via ManuallyDrop rather than joining or
      destroying them, since even destroying a POSIX mutex that looks held
      is undefined behavior.
    
    The parent is unaffected: the pthread_atfork hook only runs in the
    child. Verified with a 200-iteration fork stress test (fresh parsers
    and parsers forked mid-parse) and the full test_platform/xml/fork/
    subprocess/multiprocessing_fork test modules.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * pyexpat: implement name interning via self.intern, matching libexpat
    
    self.intern (an always-present dict by default) was stored but never
    consulted; every StartElement/EndElement/attribute name allocated a
    fresh PyStr even when the same tag or attribute name repeated across
    many sibling events. Wire it up the way libexpat does
    (PyDict_SetDefault(self->intern, name, name)): element and attribute
    *names* are now memoized so a repeated name reuses the same PyStr
    object (and its cached hash) instead of reallocating and rehashing.
    Attribute values are left out of the cache, same as libexpat.
    
    This fixes test_pyexpat's previously-xfailed InterningTest.test, which
    asserts object identity across repeated StartElementHandler/
    EndElementHandler calls.
    
    Measured on the pyperformance xml_etree parse/iterparse/generate/
    process variants: no measurable wall-clock change (the dominant cost
    there is Python-level TreeBuilder/Element bytecode execution and
    thread hand-off, not name allocation), so this is a correctness/
    feature-completion fix rather than a throughput win for that
    benchmark; kept because it is a real, previously-inert compatibility
    gap and is otherwise free.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * _elementtree: native Element, SubElement and ParseError
    
    Adds crates/stdlib/src/elementtree.rs providing the accelerator names
    xml/etree/ElementTree.py picks up at import time. Element carries the
    full API (tag/text/tail/attrib, indexing and slicing, find*/iter/
    itertext, copy/deepcopy/pickle state) with CPython's lazily created
    attrib dict and deferred text join, is subclassable, weak-referenceable
    and GC-traversable, and drops every displaced object after releasing its
    lock so a __del__ can re-enter safely.
    
    xml_etree, best of 3 (s): parse 1.64 -> 1.14, iterparse 0.68 -> 0.54,
    generate 0.44 -> 0.25, process 0.27 -> 0.16.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * _elementtree: native TreeBuilder and XMLParser
    
    TreeBuilder keeps CPython's element/data/tail bookkeeping in Rust,
    including the pull-parser event hooks _setevents installs. XMLParser
    drives a pyexpat parser whose handlers are this type's own native
    methods, so a start or end tag reaches the builder without a Python
    frame: the universal-name cache, attribute dict and TreeBuilder call all
    happen in Rust when the target is our own builder, while foreign targets
    still go through the documented start/end/data/comment/pi protocol.
    ElementTree.parse() also picks up the _parse_whole fast path.
    
    xml_etree, best of 3 (s): parse 1.14 -> 0.53, iterparse 0.54 -> 0.28,
    generate 0.25 -> 0.24, process 0.16 -> 0.16. Against the pure-Python
    baseline that is parse 3.1x, iterparse 2.4x, generate 1.8x, process
    1.7x.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * pyexpat: stop rescanning the whole line per position query
    
    LineTracker converted a TextPosition column to a byte offset by
    validating and decoding its entire buffered line tail on every query,
    and appended fed bytes to that tail one at a time. On a document that is
    one long line -- what xml.etree.tostring() writes, and what the
    xml_etree benchmark parses -- the tail is the whole read-ahead, so a
    per-event lookup became quadratic. Walk only the characters the query
    actually asks for, over the deque's two halves in place, and record fed
    bytes a line at a time. Byte indices are unchanged.
    
    xml_etree, best of 3 (s): parse 0.52 -> 0.38, iterparse 0.28 -> 0.25.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * _elementtree: charge Element.__deepcopy__ to the recursion budget
    
    A chain of elements is copied by native recursion, so a deep enough tree
    overflowed the real stack instead of raising. Wrap the copy in
    with_recursion, as CPython does, and drop the skip on
    test_deeply_nested_deepcopy for the accelerated run.
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * pyexpat: don't let a stale NeedMore end a Parse() early
    
    The parser thread reports NeedMore when it finds the feed buffer empty
    and parks. If it reached that point before the chunk Parse() was about
    to push became visible -- which it does whenever the thread happens to
    be scheduled first, so more often under load -- pump() consumed that
    report as if it answered the new chunk and handed control back to Python
    having dispatched nothing, silently losing the whole document.
    
    Stamp each NeedMore with the push count observed when the buffer was
    found empty and ignore reports older than the push being pumped. The
    push notifies the condvar under the same mutex the report was sent
    from, so the parser thread is guaranteed to wake and report again.
    
    Fixes an intermittent failure of test_pyexpat's InterningTest under
    parallel load (about 5 losses per 20000 parses before, none after).
    
    Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
    
    * Restore xml.etree test markers instead of skipTest in bodies
    
    Assisted-by: Grok:4.6
    
    * Clone state before try_to_bool in pickle load_build
    
    try_to_bool takes the object by value, and load_build still needs the
    state dict afterwards.
    
    Assisted-by: Grok:4.6
    
    * Drop decorative section-separator comments
    
    Assisted-by: Grok:4.6
    
    * Represent pickle opcodes as an enum
    
    Assisted-by: Grok:4.6
    
    * Use full paths for thread-only types in pyexpat
    
    Assisted-by: Grok:4.6
    
